{"@context":["https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/ns/content/v1.jsonld",{"@base":"https://litlfred.github.io/folio/","folio-assistant-sci":"https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant-sci/v0/ns/"}],"@id":"papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/def-two-q-adic-valuation","@type":["folio-assistant-sci:Definition","doco:Section"],"label":"def:two-q-adic-valuation","kind":"definition","title":"The $[2]_q$-adic valuation on the Skein module","tags":["collatz","contact-order","adic-valuation","skein-module","divisor"],"leanRef":"qou:QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.TwoQAdicValuation","text":"two-q-adic-valuation.md","leanSource":"two-q-adic-valuation.lean","provenance":"authored","html":"<a id=\"def:two-q-adic-valuation\"></a><p>For an element <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">P \\in \\mathcal{S}(q) \\cong \\mathbb{Z}[q^{\\pm 1}]</code> of the Skein module, define the <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">[2]_q</code>-<strong>adic valuation</strong>:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-math math-display\">v_{[2]}(P) := \\max\\bigl\\{k \\geq 0 : [2]_q^k \\mid P \\text{ in } \\mathbb{Z}[q^{\\pm 1}]\\bigr\\}.</code></pre>\n<p>This counts how many times <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">[2]_q = q + 1</code> divides <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">P</code> in the Laurent polynomial ring. It is the <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">q</code>-analog of the <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">2</code>-adic valuation <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">v_2(n)</code> that counts trailing zeros in the binary expansion of an integer.</p>\n<p>The contact order of <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">P</code> with the <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">[2]_q</code>-divisor <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">D_{[2]} = \\{q : [2]_q = 0\\} = \\{-1\\}</code> is <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">v_{[2]}(P)</code>: the order to which <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">P(q)</code> vanishes at <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">q = -1</code>. High contact order indicates the orbit grazes the divisor closely (many halvings can be extracted).</p>\n<p>At <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">q = 1</code>, <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">[2]_1 = 2</code>, and <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">v_{[2]}(n) = v_2(n)</code> recovers ordinary <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">2</code>-adic valuation.</p>\n","source":"folio/quantum-observable-universe/appendix-surreals/two-q-adic-valuation.md","heading":"Definition","number":"28.9.13","page":"b/def-two-q-adic-valuation","qa":{"counts":{"pass":82,"fail":0,"warn":0,"na":8,"unknown":0,"stale":63},"milnor":{"total":2,"notPassing":0}},"lean":{"expected":true,"ref":"qou:QOU.Core.AlgebraicSubstrate.TwoQAdicValuation","via":"sibling","path":"folio/quantum-observable-universe/appendix-surreals/two-q-adic-valuation.lean","compiles":"unchecked","sorry":false,"sorryBasis":"text"}}
